{"qid":"Q238445","names":["Emirate of Granada"],"title":"Emirate of Granada","endonym":{"native":"إمارة غرﻧﺎﻃﺔ","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"The Emirate of Granada was the last Muslim state on the Iberian Peninsula, founded in 1238 by Muhammad I ibn Nasr, who established the Nasrid dynasty and began construction of the Alhambra palace-citadel above the city. Surviving for over two and a half centuries as a tributary of Castile, Granada owed its longevity to its mountainous terrain, the military competence of successive emirs, and Castile's frequent preoccupations elsewhere—its economy was sustained by silk production, sugar cultivation, and luxury exports across the Strait of Gibraltar. Internal succession struggles in the 1480s allowed Ferdinand and Isabella to press a systematic decade-long conquest; the last emir, Muhammad XII (Boabdil), surrendered the city on 2 January 1492, completing the Reconquista.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Granada's boundaries are among the best-documented of any Iberian medieval polity, defined by contemporary Castilian-Granadan treaties and documented in both Christian and Arabic sources.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Emirate of Granada","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirate_of_Granada"},{"label":"Britannica: Granada, emirate, Spain","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Granada-emirate-Spain"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Nasrid dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasrid_dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Alhambra","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alhambra"}],"britannica":"place/Granada-historical-kingdom-Spain","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Emir